lesson:
A dynamic array keeps everything in one block. That's great for reading, but inserting or removing in the middle means shifting everything after it, and growing means copying. A **linked list** takes the opposite approach: every item lives in its own small heap block, called a **node**, and each node points to the next one.

```
typedef struct Node {
    int value;
    struct Node *next;   /* NULL at the end */
} Node;
```

The list itself is just a pointer to the first node, the **head**. An empty list is `head == NULL`.

```
head -> [4 | *] -> [7 | *] -> [9 | NULL]
```

**Walking the list** is the one loop you'll write over and over:

```
for (Node *n = head; n != NULL; n = n->next)
    printf("%d\n", n->value);
```

**Push to the front** is instant, no matter how long the list is: make a node, point it at the old head, and it becomes the new head.

```
Node *push_front(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    if (n == NULL) return head;
    n->value = value;
    n->next = head;
    return n;          /* the new head */
}

head = push_front(head, 5);
```

Notice the function *returns* the new head and the caller stores it. Forget `head =` and the new node is lost.

**Push to the back**: walk to the last node (the one whose `next` is NULL) and link the new node there. If the list was empty, the new node *is* the head.

**Removing** a node means making the node before it skip over it: `prev->next = n->next;` then free `n`. Removing the head is the special case: the new head is `head->next`.

**Freeing the whole list** has a trap. This is wrong:

```
for (Node *n = head; n != NULL; n = n->next)
    free(n);           /* n->next is read AFTER n is freed */
```

Save the next pointer first:

```
while (head != NULL) {
    Node *next = head->next;
    free(head);
    head = next;
}
```

**When to use which?** Arrays win for looping fast and jumping to item 500. Lists win when items come and go in the middle all the time, or when you can't afford a big copy while growing. Real uses: the queue of patients at a clinic desk, a game's list of active bullets, the free-memory list inside `malloc` itself, the task list in a small robot's scheduler.

example.code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node { int value; struct Node *next; } Node;

Node *push_front(Node *head, int value)
{
    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    if (n == NULL) return head;
    n->value = value;
    n->next = head;
    return n;
}

int main(void)
{
    Node *orders = NULL;                  /* empty list */
    orders = push_front(orders, 103);
    orders = push_front(orders, 102);
    orders = push_front(orders, 101);

    for (Node *n = orders; n != NULL; n = n->next)
        printf("order %d\n", n->value);

    while (orders != NULL) {              /* free every node */
        Node *next = orders->next;
        free(orders);
        orders = next;
    }
    return 0;
}

